## TL;DR  
The “spiky‑mountain” you see is caused by **MapLibre decoding the Terrarium PNGs with the wrong colour‑encoding** (it assumes the Mapbox *terrain‑rgb* format by default).  
Add the `encoding: "terrarium"` property (and a few other small tweaks) to the DEM source and the terrain will render correctly.

---

## 1️⃣ What’s happening under the hood  

| Tile format | How the height is stored | How MapLibre expects it by default |
|-------------|--------------------------|------------------------------------|
| **Mapbox `terrain-rgb`** | `height = (R * 256 + G + B/256) – 32768` | **Default** – MapLibre assumes this. |
| **AWS “Terrarium”** | `height = (R * 256 + G + B/256) – 32768` **but** the PNGs are *encoded* exactly the same way, **only the source is different**. | If you don’t tell MapLibre the source is “terrarium”, it still uses the default *Mapbox* decoder, which interprets the bytes incorrectly → huge jumps, vertical walls, flat tops. |

Because the decoder is wrong, the elevation values become wildly out‑of‑range, which is why you see:

* **Violently spiky surfaces** – huge positive/negative deltas between adjacent pixels.  
* **Sheer walls at coastlines** – the decoder reads the “sea‑level” pixels as a huge step.  
* **Flat peaks** – many pixels collapse to the same (wrong) value.

The fix is simply to tell MapLibre which encoding to use.

---

## 2️⃣ Minimal, working example  

Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste** snippet that works with the current (as of 2026) MapLibre GL JS v2.x.  
It includes:

* The `encoding: "terrarium"` flag.  
* `maxzoom: 12` (the highest zoom the AWS tiles provide).  
* A small `exaggeration` (optional) so you can see the relief more clearly.  
* A `hillshade` layer (optional) to make the terrain look nicer.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8" />
<title>MapLibre + AWS Terrarium DEM</title>
<meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no" />
<link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
<style>
  body { margin:0; padding:0; }
  #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣ Initialise the map (any style you like)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // simple OSM basemap
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574], // San Francisco (just an example)
    zoom: 10,
    pitch: 60,          // tilt the camera so you can see the 3‑D
    bearing: -17,
    antialias: true     // required for smooth terrain shading
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣ Add the Terrarium DEM source
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    map.addSource('aws-terrain', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      tiles: [
        // The AWS Terrarium tiles (public, no API key needed)
        'https://s3.amazonaws.com/elevation-tiles-prod/terrarium/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'
      ],
      tileSize: 256,
      maxzoom: 12,          // highest zoom level the source provides
      encoding: 'terrarium' // <<< THIS IS THE crucial line
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣ Enable terrain rendering
    // -------------------------------------------------
    map.setTerrain({
      source: 'aws-terrain',
      exaggeration: 1.5   // optional – makes mountains pop a bit
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣ (Optional) Add a hillshade layer for visual polish
    // -------------------------------------------------
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      source: 'aws-terrain',
      type: 'hillshade',
      paint: {
        // Adjust these to taste
        'hillshade-shadow-color': '#000',
        'hillshade-highlight-color': '#fff',
        'hillshade-accent-color': '#f0f0f0'
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What changed compared to your original source?

```json
{
  "type": "raster-dem",
  "tiles": [
    "https://s3.amazonaws.com/elevation-tiles-prod/terrarium/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"
  ],
  "tileSize": 256,
  "maxzoom": 12,
  "encoding": "terrarium"   // ← NEW
}
```

* **`encoding: "terrarium"`** tells MapLibre to use the Terrarium decoder (the same formula as Mapbox’s terrain‑rgb, but the source is different).  
* **`maxzoom`** is not strictly required, but it prevents the library from requesting non‑existent higher‑zoom tiles (which would otherwise return 404s).  

---

## 3️⃣ Why the other properties matter  

| Property | Why you need it (or why it’s optional) |
|----------|----------------------------------------|
| `tileSize: 256` | Terrarium tiles are 256 px; MapLibre defaults to 512 for raster‑dem, so we set it explicitly. |
| `maxzoom: 12` | The AWS dataset stops at zoom 12. If you omit this, MapLibre may request zoom 13+ and get 404s, which can cause a brief “no‑data” flash. |
| `exaggeration` | Not required, but a small factor (1‑2) makes the relief easier to see, especially at lower zooms. |
| `antialias: true` (map option) | Enables smoother shading of the terrain surface. |
| `hillshade` layer | Purely cosmetic – adds shadows/highlights that make the 3‑D look more realistic. |

---

## 4️⃣ Common pitfalls & how to avoid them  

| Symptom | Typical cause | Fix |
|---------|----------------|-----|
| **Spiky, vertical walls** | Wrong `encoding` (default Mapbox) | Add `"encoding": "terrarium"` |
| **Flat “plateaus” where peaks should be** | Using the Terrarium tiles but forgetting `tileSize: 256` (MapLibre assumes 512) | Set `tileSize: 256` |
| **No terrain at all** | `map.setTerrain` called **before** the source is added / before `load` event | Call `setTerrain` inside `map.on('load', …)` or after `addSource` finishes. |
| **White/black “no‑data” squares** | Requesting zoom levels > 12 (tiles don’t exist) | Set `maxzoom: 12` or limit the map’s `maxZoom`. |
| **Performance lag on mobile** | Very high `exaggeration` + high `maxzoom` + no `antialias` flag | Keep exaggeration modest, limit `maxzoom`, enable `antialias`. |
| **CORS errors** | Using a custom tile server that doesn’t send `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin` | The AWS S3 bucket already sends the correct CORS headers, so you’re safe as long as you use the exact URL above. |

---

## 5️⃣ Quick sanity‑check: Verify the DEM values yourself  

If you want to be absolutely sure the decoder is working, you can fetch a single tile and decode a pixel manually:

```js
async function fetchTerrariumTile(z, x, y) {
  const url = `https://s3.amazonaws.com/elevation-tiles-prod/terrarium/${z}/${x}/${y}.png`;
  const img = await fetch(url).then(r => r.blob()).then(URL.createObjectURL).then(src => {
    return new Promise(resolve => {
      const i = new Image();
      i.crossOrigin = 'anonymous';
      i.onload = () => resolve(i);
      i.src = src;
    });
  });

  const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
  canvas.width = img.width;
  canvas.height = img.height;
  const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
  ctx.drawImage(img, 0, 0);
  const data = ctx.getImageData(0, 0, 1, 1).data; // top‑left pixel
  const [R, G, B] = data;
  const elevation = (R * 256 + G + B / 256) - 32768;
  console.log(`Pixel (0,0) elevation = ${elevation} m`);
}
fetchTerrariumTile(10, 163, 395); // example tile over the Sierra Nevada
```

You should see a sensible elevation (e.g., ~1500 m). If you get something like `-32768` or a huge positive number, the decoder is still wrong.

---

## 6️⃣ Going further – other terrain sources  

* **Mapbox Terrain‑RGB** – use `"encoding": "mapbox"` (or omit the property, because it’s the default).  
* **USGS 3DEP** – also available as Terrarium PNGs; same `encoding` flag works.  
* **Custom DEMs** – you can serve your own GeoTIFFs via a TileJSON raster‑dem source; just make sure the encoding matches the pixel format.

---

## 7️⃣ TL;DR checklist  

1. **Add `encoding: "terrarium"`** to the DEM source.  
2. Set **`tileSize: 256`** (Terrarium tiles are 256 px).  
3. Set **`maxzoom: 12`** (the highest zoom the AWS tiles provide).  
4. Call **`map.setTerrain({source: 'your-source-id', exaggeration: …})`** *after* the source is added (usually inside `map.on('load')`).  
5. (Optional) Add a **hillshade** layer for nicer shading.  

That’s it – your mountains should now look like mountains, not a jagged nightmare. Happy mapping! 🚀

