Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste ready** example that shows how to put your own 256 px JPEG tile service  

```
https://tiles.example.com/imagery/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg
```  

as the **bottom (base) layer** of a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1️⃣  HTML skeleton

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre – Satellite base layer</title>

  <!-- MapLibre GL JS (latest as of 2024) -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; left:0; right:0; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Create a *blank* style (no background, no layers)
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  const emptyStyle = {
    version: 8,
    name: "Empty style – we’ll add our own raster source",
    sources: {},          // we’ll fill this later
    layers: []            // we’ll fill this later
  };

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣  Initialise the map
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',          // id of the div above
    style: emptyStyle,         // start with the empty style
    center: [0, 0],            // lon, lat – change to your area of interest
    zoom: 2,                   // initial zoom
    hash: true                 // optional – shows lat/lng/zoom in URL
  });

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣  When the map is ready, add the raster source + layer
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    // ---- 4a. Add the raster source --------------------------------
    map.addSource('satellite-imagery', {
      type: 'raster',
      tiles: [
        // The {z}/{x}/{y} placeholders are automatically replaced by MapLibre
        'https://tiles.example.com/imagery/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'
      ],
      tileSize: 256,          // your tiles are 256 px × 256 px
      // optional – tell MapLibre the zoom range your tiles cover
      minzoom: 0,
      maxzoom: 18,
      // optional – attribution that will appear in the UI
      attribution: '© Example Satellite Provider'
    });

    // ---- 4b. Add the raster layer (bottom layer) -----------------
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'satellite-base',
      type: 'raster',
      source: 'satellite-imagery',
      // The layer is added *first*, so it sits at the bottom.
      // If you later add other layers, they will appear on top.
      paint: {
        // You can control opacity here if you ever want it semi‑transparent
        // 'raster-opacity': 1.0
      }
    });

    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // 5️⃣  (Optional) Add a simple vector overlay to prove it works
    // --------------------------------------------------------------
    // Example: add a GeoJSON point at the map centre
    map.addSource('center-point', {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: {
        type: 'FeatureCollection',
        features: [{
          type: 'Feature',
          geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [0, 0] },
          properties: { name: 'Map centre' }
        }]
      }
    });

    map.addLayer({
      id: 'center-point-circle',
      type: 'circle',
      source: 'center-point',
      paint: {
        'circle-radius': 6,
        'circle-color': '#ff0000',
        'circle-stroke-width': 2,
        'circle-stroke-color': '#ffffff'
      }
    });
  });
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

---

## What the code does, step‑by‑step

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **1️⃣ HTML skeleton** | Loads MapLibre GL JS (via CDN) and creates a full‑screen `<div id="map">`. | Gives you a container for the map and the library itself. |
| **2️⃣ Empty style** | Starts with a style that has **no sources or layers**. | Guarantees your raster source is the *first* layer, i.e. the bottom of the stack. |
| **3️⃣ Map creation** | `new maplibregl.Map({ … })` creates the map instance. | You can set the initial centre/zoom, enable URL hash, etc. |
| **4a️⃣ Add raster source** | `map.addSource('satellite-imagery', {type:'raster', …})`. | Tells MapLibre where to fetch the tiles, the tile size, zoom limits, and attribution. |
| **4b️⃣ Add raster layer** | `map.addLayer({id:'satellite-base', type:'raster', source:'satellite-imagery'})`. | The layer is added **first**, so it becomes the base map. |
| **5️⃣ Optional overlay** | Adds a tiny red dot at `[0,0]` to prove other layers sit on top. | Demonstrates that your raster is truly the bottom layer. |

---

## Things to double‑check before you go live

| Issue | What to verify |
|-------|----------------|
| **CORS** | The tile server must send `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin:*` (or your domain) headers, otherwise the browser will block the images. |
| **HTTPS** | If your page is served over HTTPS, the tile URLs must also be HTTPS, otherwise the browser will block mixed‑content requests. |
| **Tile coverage** | Make sure the server actually has tiles for the zoom range you set (`minzoom`/`maxzoom`). Missing tiles will appear as transparent gaps. |
| **Attribution** | Some providers require a visible attribution. The `attribution` field in the source definition will automatically appear in the default MapLibre UI (the little “i” button). |
| **Performance** | If you expect heavy traffic, consider enabling HTTP/2 or a CDN in front of the tile server to reduce latency. |
| **Cache‑busting** | If you ever change the tile set, you can add a query string (e.g., `?v=2024-08`) to force browsers to fetch the new tiles. |

---

## Alternative: Using a **style JSON** instead of `addSource`/`addLayer`

If you prefer to keep everything in a single JSON file (e.g., you host a static style on a CDN), you can write:

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "satellite-imagery": {
      "type": "raster",
      "tiles": ["https://tiles.example.com/imagery/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg"],
      "tileSize": 256,
      "minzoom": 0,
      "maxzoom": 18,
      "attribution": "© Example Satellite Provider"
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "satellite-base",
      "type": "raster",
      "source": "satellite-imagery"
    }
    // …any other layers you want to add later
  ]
}
```

Then initialise the map with:

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://my.cdn.com/styles/satellite-base.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

Both approaches are equivalent; pick the one that fits your workflow.

---

### 🎉 You’re done!

Open the HTML file in a browser, and you should see your satellite imagery filling the whole viewport, with a tiny red dot at the centre (or any other layers you add later). From here you can keep adding vector layers, controls, pop‑ups, etc., all on top of the raster base you just wired in. Happy mapping!
